the work considers the damped pinney equation , defined as the model arising when a linear in velocity damping term is included in the pinney equation . A general solution of the problem with arbitrary damping parameter has been reported in. We restrict ourselves to the case of weak damping to compare our results with those published in.

In this paper, a general analysis of the damped pinney equation is provided. We consider a simple one-dimensional model and determine the form of a general solution in terms of hypergeometric functions. As a consequence, we find that the model’s fundamental property of “self-oscillation” is dependent on the magnitude parameter, as shown in Theorem \[thm:MainThm\]. Hence, if $\lambda$ is chosen appropriately (i.e. if the magnitude parameter is chosen to be sufficiently large), then pinney equation behaves as a damped pendulum equation with damping. In particular, for $m > \cos\delta(2p-|\gamma|)$, the pinney equation self-oscillates even though the pinney equation has no stable equilibrium point corresponding to a harmonic motion. The theorem also shows how to obtain a complete solution and it gives us a tool to determine when the damped pinney equation will exhibit self-oscillation. This work may shed light on the damped pinney equation and pinney equation theory in general.

