Cyclic peptides have remarkable properties that differ from regular linear peptides. The first property is their ability to resist protease degradation, hence to have a better bioavailability. In addition, cyclization forms cyclic structures that have low entropic penalty, and hence, cyclic peptides often have higher activity and stability than their linear counterparts.

