krppel - like factors ( klfs ) , members of the zinc - finger family of transcription factors capable of binding gc - rich sequences , have emerged as critical regulators of important functions all over the body . they are characterised by a highly conserved c - terminal dna - binding motif containing three c2h2 zinc - finger domains , with variable n - terminal regulatory domains . currently , there are 17 klfs annotated in the human genome . for many years, we had been investigating the role of klf6 in the mouse retina, which is expressed in specific retinal neuronal populations and plays a role in the proliferation and differentiation of these cells. In this study, we identify that the human ortholog of klf6, klf4, regulates the differentiation of melanoblasts. We show that expression of human klf4 is induced during human melanoblast differentiation and that its overexpression in mouse melanoma cells increases their differentiation in vitro and inhibits tumour growth in vivo. thus, this study establishes klf4 as a human regulator of melanoblast differentiation and highlights it as a new target for the treatment of melanoma. In addition, by a differential gene expression approach, we show that human klf4 regulates melanoblast differentiation indirectly, targeting genes associated with cAMP - activity. To the best of our knowledge, this work represents the first functional characterisation of human klf4 in mammalian cells.

