we recently reported that depletion of p62 in the background of human epidermal growth factor receptor 2 ( her2 ) overexpression sensitizes mammary tumor cells to amino acid deprivation , abolishes cellular transformation in vitro , and suppresses mammary tumorigenesis in vivo . In our current study we have analyzed these effects further in a genetically engineered context and identified a specific function of p62 as a molecular link that is necessary to sustain amino acid homeostasis. This includes amino acid uptake and lysosomal degradation of amino acid analogs. Amino acid-deplete media induce autophagy in cells and this is blocked by downregulation of mTOR. Upon amino acid deprivation p62-driven degradation of the essential amino acid transporter ASCT2 is impaired and its expression is essential for amino acid uptake. This results in accumulation of amino acids via the autophagy-lysosomal system and activation of mammalian target of rapamycin ( mTOR), which blocks autophagy and induces cancer-relevant gene expression in mammary tumor cells. These findings have implications for therapy of mammary cancer and other diseases, as selective inhibition of p62 or ASCT2 might be a useful strategy for the treatment of cancers with aberrant amino acids homeostasis.

