we present , as a very general method , an effective field theory to analyze models defined over small - world networks . even if the exactness of the method is limited to the paramagnetic regions and to some special limits , it gives the exact critical behavior and the exact critical surfaces and percolation thresholds , and provide a clear and immediate ( also in terms of calculation ) insight of the physics . the underlying structure of the non random part of the model , _  i.e.  _, being a superposition of ferromagnetic couplings, determines the existence of two new stable fixed points and the possibility of two dimensional percolation. we have analyzed one of these models, which for large - world networks exhibits critical slowing down, and we argue that such a percolation dynamics would be at the origin of the observed slowing down in the dynamics of protein folding.

